   "exam": [
    "When a blood vessel is cut, the blood clots. Clotting has two roles: it _prevents blood loss_, and it _prevents the entry of pathogens_ through the wound.",
    "_Platelets_ are involved in clotting. At a damaged vessel they stick to the edges of the wound and to each other, and release chemicals that start the clotting reactions.",
    {
     "text": "How the clot forms. Plasma carries a soluble protein called _fibrinogen_. At a wound it is converted into _fibrin_, which is _insoluble_. The fibrin forms long threads that make a _mesh_ across the wound. The mesh traps red blood cells and platelets, forming a _clot_. The clot dries and hardens into a scab, and the skin repairs itself underneath.",
     "sup": true
    },
    {
     "text": "The conversion is done by an enzyme, thrombin, which is itself produced at the wound by a chain of reactions. A person with haemophilia lacks one of the proteins in that chain, so their blood clots very slowly.",
     "ext": true
    }
   ],
